    "id": "domain-aggregates",
    "title": "Aggregates, invariants & domain events",
    "module": "Design a working feature",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "minutes": 20,
    "summary": "Define the smallest useful consistency boundary for business behavior.",
    "concepts": [
      [
        "An aggregate guards related state",
        "An aggregate root controls access to a cluster whose invariants must hold together. For an order, line items and total calculation may belong inside one boundary. Making the entire business one aggregate creates contention; making every field separate can make essential invariants impossible to enforce locally."
      ],
      [
        "Reference other aggregates deliberately",
        "Use stable identities rather than unrestricted object graphs when crossing boundaries. Cross-aggregate workflows may require eventual coordination, a stronger transaction, or a revised boundary. A domain-model diagram does not itself guarantee database isolation."
      ],
      [
        "Domain events record meaningful facts",
        "An event such as OrderConfirmed expresses a domain change. Publish it reliably with the corresponding state change, often through an outbox. Distinguish internal domain events from external integration contracts, which need versioning and consumer compatibility."
      ]
    ],
    "example": "An Order root owns line items and refuses confirmation when empty. A WarehouseStock aggregate owns available inventory. “Confirm order” cannot assume reading both objects in memory makes reservation atomic. The application service coordinates the reservation workflow and handles failure according to the chosen persistence boundaries.",
    "walkthrough": [
      [
        "Write the invariant",
        "For the order: total equals the sum of valid line totals, and a confirmed order has at least one line."
      ],
      [
        "Control mutations",
        "Only root operations change lines or confirmation state; callers cannot mutate the internal list directly."
      ],
      [
        "Coordinate outside the boundary",
        "Reserve stock through its authority and persist reliable events/outcomes instead of silently spanning unrelated objects."
      ]
    ],
    "code": "// Java-oriented contract sketch\ninterface Orders {\n    Order load(OrderId id);\n    void save(Order order, long expectedVersion);\n}\n// Order.addLine(...) and Order.confirm() preserve local rules.\n// Inventory ownership is coordinated through its own contract.\n// Save + outbox event share the appropriate local transaction.",
    "complexity": "Aggregate boundaries are hypotheses about consistency and change. Revisit them when contention, transactions, or business rules show a mismatch.",
    "pitfalls": [
      "Treating an aggregate as just a database table.",
      "Exposing mutable child entities that bypass the root.",
      "Publishing an event before the state transaction can still fail."
    ],
    "practice": "An aggregate locks all inventory for an entire warehouse on every purchase. What problem might its boundary create?",
    "solution": "Unrelated products contend on one consistency boundary, reducing concurrency and increasing conflict retries. If invariants permit, use smaller stock units such as warehouse+SKU and coordinate cross-SKU purchases explicitly. Do not split merely for speed if a true atomic multi-item invariant is required.",
