    "concepts": [
      [
        "Functional dependencies capture rules",
        "If customer_id determines customer_email, the email belongs to the customer fact. Repeating it on every order creates several places that can disagree. Dependencies describe the domain, not merely coincidences in one small sample of data."
      ],
      [
        "Normalize to protect meaning",
        "Normalization separates facts so inserts, updates, and deletes do not create avoidable anomalies. First normal form is commonly taught through atomic attributes and relational rows; higher forms address dependencies on parts of composite keys and on non-key attributes. Use the actual candidate keys when reasoning."
      ],
      [
        "Denormalization is a maintained projection",
        "Duplicating a derived value can accelerate a measured read path, but specify the source of truth and update mechanism. A snapshot can be intentionally historical—an order’s purchased price should not necessarily change when the product’s current price changes. Not all repeated data is an anomaly."
      ]
    ],
    "example": "Order rows repeat customer_email. Changing an email in only one row makes the customer appear to have different current emails. Move the current email to Customers. Keep an explicit billing_email_at_purchase snapshot only if the business needs historical evidence; name it so readers understand its semantics.",
